---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { allParks, L } from '../lib/parks';
import coverageFile from '../data/coverage.json';

// Running counts, worked out from the playground files on every build. Counties come from src/data/coverage.json,
// which the "Update nearest toilets" workflow refreshes whenever playgrounds change.
const parks = await allParks();
const total = parks.length;
const checked = parks.filter((p) => p.checked).length;
const counties = coverageFile.counties;
const nations = coverageFile.nations;
const list = (xs: string[]) => (xs.length > 1 ? `${xs.slice(0, -1).join(', ')} and ${xs.at(-1)}` : xs[0] ?? '');
const orgs = Object.entries(L.memberships)
  .map(([k, name]) => ({ name, n: parks.filter((p) => p.membership === k).length }))
  .filter((o) => o.n > 0);
const memberTotal = orgs.reduce((a, o) => a + o.n, 0);
const n = (x: number) => x.toLocaleString('en-GB');
---
<Base title="About our data">
  <h1>About our data</h1>
  <section class="panel stats" aria-labelledby="sofar" style="box-shadow:none;max-width:70ch;margin-bottom:20px">
    <h2 id="sofar">Swing By so far</h2>
    <ul class="stat-row">
      <li><strong>{n(total)}</strong> playgrounds</li>
      <li><strong>{counties.length}</strong> English counties{nations.length > 0 && <>, plus parts of {list(nations.map((x) => x.name))}</>}</li>
      <li><strong>{orgs.length}</strong> membership organisations</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>{n(checked)} of the {n(total)} playgrounds have been checked against the council's or operator's own website. The other {n(total - checked)} are marked "Unverified".</p>
      {counties.length > 0 && <p>Counties we cover, most playgrounds first: {list(counties.map((c) => `${c.name} (${n(c.playgrounds)})`))}.{nations.length > 0 && <> We also list {list(nations.map((x) => `${n(x.playgrounds)} in ${x.name}`))}.</>} A county counts once we list at least three playgrounds there.</p>}
      <p>Play areas at {n(memberTotal)} places members get into: {list(orgs.map((o) => `${o.name} (${n(o.n)})`))}. See <a href="/memberships/">Memberships</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <div class="prose panel" style="box-shadow:none;max-width:70ch">
    <h2>What you'll know before you go</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Toilets and baby change:</strong> whether a playground has its own toilets, whether they have baby change, and where the nearest public toilets are.</li>
      <li><strong>Free or paid, at a glance:</strong> places that charge for entry are marked with <span class="pound" aria-hidden="true">£</span>, and you can leave them out.</li>
      <li><strong>Council-checked, or clearly marked:</strong> each playground says where its details came from and when we last checked them, and anything we haven't checked yet is marked "Unverified".</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>Where the details come from</h2>
    <p>Swing By lists playgrounds using information published by councils, park operators and membership organisations. Each playground shows where its details came from and when we last checked them.</p>
    <p>Places marked "Unverified" have not been checked against an official source yet. Many come from <a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright">OpenStreetMap</a>, which we use where a council doesn't publish a list we can check. Treat their details as a guide only. Parents can tick off what a playground has on its page; their answers only show once two different parents agree. The same goes for pins: if a pin is in the wrong place, parents can show us where the playground really is, and we move it once two different parents agree on the spot.</p>
    <p>Opening times can change and many parks lock their gates at dusk. Check before a long journey.</p>
    <p>Places marked <span class="pound" aria-hidden="true">£</span> Paid entry charge to get in, or sit inside somewhere that does, such as a National Trust garden or a stately home. Members of the organisation may get in free. Tick "Free entry only" to leave them out. Paid parking alone doesn't count as paid entry.</p>
    <p><strong>Safety:</strong> always check equipment before children use it. If something is broken or dangerous, report it to the council or operator, for example through FixMyStreet. In an emergency, call 999.</p>
    <p>We don't use photos that show identifiable children.</p>
    <p>Maps use <a href="https://openfreemap.org">OpenFreeMap</a> tiles and data © <a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright">OpenStreetMap contributors</a>. Postcode lookups use <a href="https://postcodes.io">postcodes.io</a>, with data from Ordnance Survey and the ONS.</p>
    <p>Nearest public toilets come from the <a href="https://www.toiletmap.org.uk/dataset">Toilet Map</a> (CC BY 4.0). We work out which toilets are closest to each playground; we don't change the Toilet Map's details. Baby change at a playground's own toilets comes from the council or operator, or from the Toilet Map where we say so.</p>
    <h2 id="adverts">Adverts</h2>
    <p>The advert near the bottom of each page links to things for days out on Amazon. As an Amazon Associate, Swing By earns from qualifying purchases, which helps pay for the site. It doesn't cost you anything extra. The advert is a plain link: nothing loads from Amazon and no Amazon cookies are set until you click it.</p>
    <h2 id="cookies">Cookies and analytics</h2>
    <p>If you say yes, we use Google Analytics to count visits and see which pages are useful. It sets cookies and sends Google your browser details, the pages you look at and an approximate location based on your IP address. We don't use it for advertising. If you say no, Google Analytics isn't loaded at all.</p>
    <p>If you say yes, we also tell Google Analytics what people search for, so we can see where to add playgrounds and how to make search better. When you search by postcode we send it without its last two characters, so "AL1 3AF" is sent as "AL1 3", and never the whole postcode. When you search by town we send the town's name. Words you type into Advanced search are sent too, with any full postcode cut back in the same way. We don't send your location when you tap Near me.</p>
    <p>Your choice is saved in your browser. You can change it at any time with "Cookie settings" at the bottom of every page.</p>
    <p>Some features use a cookie of their own because they need it to work: the vote for where we go next remembers your vote, telling us what a playground has, or where it really is, remembers your answers so you can change them, and signing in to edit content remembers that you're signing in.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
